Peter Snelling writes
>I think the main purpose of the WOW in the castle (or under a boulder as it
>used to be in some older version), was to allow you to pick up the few things
>you really need before going to Hell.  For example, if you have teleport and
>not teleport control, the game is pretty frustrating.  Instead of searching
>on and on for tengu's or rings, now is you chance to get it, and just go on
>with the game. (or magic resistant armor, or whatever you still need).

This is exactly why I don't like WoW's. Being forced to do without
our favorite item(s) is currently the only way to achieve any sort of
character diversity in the latter half of the game. I would vote to remove
wishes altogether but I'm probably in the minority (and this isn't a
democracy.) To Brian Galle who would remind us that we can always choose
not to wish for anything I would just say this. I think that self imposed
restrictions, while useful for providing variety of play, can not generate
the kind of excitement one experiences when the restrictions are put
into the code by the mighty DEV team. :)

One question: Has anyone ascended without ever using a unicorn horn? I
have ascended twice (an S and an H, rather unremarkable games) and
cannot imagine winning without one.

For discussion: Are there any items (other than candles etc.) which anyone
feels are truly indispensible?

